From 06d7c405a5b5dd2faa6f6baecbbb3838296b1e10 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: KnutMann <knutmann@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 04:50:38 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 24/39] Answer the review of the history fetch

Nine findings from an adversarial reading, all of them about the same
thing: a conversation coming into being is a weaker signal than it
looked, and the code around it took too much on trust.

The guard against a message bringing its own conversation into being
covered only text; an arriving attachment walked straight past it. It
now wraps the handling of every message kind, which is where the
conversation is created whatever the kind. Coming online creates whole
conversations by itself, one per group where chats are joined
automatically, so requests wait fifteen seconds after the connection is
up before any of them counts as the user opening something.

The anchored request took the conversation from the cached message as
well as the message itself. The cache records a chat as the message path
left it, which for a status broadcast is one device of a contact rather
than the conversation, and that would have gone out as the chat to fetch.
The conversation asked about is now the one that was parsed.

An edited message parses under the id of the message it edits, which is
the right id but would have put two lines on the page claiming to be the
same message. The later one wins. Locations and polls, which the live
path spells out in full, were dropped from the replay without trace and
are now named. The list of conversations asked about recently is pruned
rather than kept for the life of the connection. SETTINGS.md says what
the code does, including the two things a user cannot otherwise find
out: that a stretch of history is handed over once, and that a device
must have said at linking time that it can receive any.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
