From d8319c1decc2cc3d766ef6bb239aebe048c5b641 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: KnutMann <knutmann@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 20:12:22 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 27/39] The plug-in shares Adium's libpurple instead of
 bringing its own

Adium loads its libpurple and glib from frameworks inside the app bundle.
This plug-in's link line records the build-tree paths instead, so inside
Adium it dragged in a SECOND copy of each and registered its protocol there,
where the application never looks. Nothing fails loudly on that road: the
plug-in probes cleanly, loads cleanly, reports no error, and WhatsApp simply
never connects, because the libpurple Adium asks for the protocol is not the
libpurple the protocol registered with.

Proven outside the application with a harness that mirrors libpurple's own
plug-in directory scan against the bundle's frameworks: linked at build-tree
paths, the plug-in loads and purple_find_prpl still answers nothing; with the
references rewritten to the bundle's frameworks, the same binary is found.
Every other protocol plug-in shipped beside it already points at the bundle's
frameworks, which is what made the difference visible.

The rewrite is now a post-build step of the target itself, followed by the
ad-hoc codesign that keeps the application's copy phase from stripping the Go
runtime out of the binary. gdk-pixbuf deliberately keeps its Homebrew path
for now: it exchanges no libpurple or GObject state with the host and has
worked that way all along; folding it into the bundle is the known,
separately-tracked duplicate-library chore.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 glue/CMakeLists.txt | 18 ++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 18 insertions(+)

diff --git a/glue/CMakeLists.txt b/glue/CMakeLists.txt
index 328b9c1..76566c9 100644
--- a/glue/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/glue/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -35,6 +35,24 @@ if (WIN32 AND CMAKE_C_COMPILER_ID STREQUAL GNU AND CMAKE_C_COMPILER_VERSION VERS
 endif()
 if (APPLE)
     target_link_libraries(${TARGET_NAME} PRIVATE "-framework CoreFoundation" "-framework Security" resolv)
+
+    # Adium loads its OWN libpurple and glib from the app bundle. Left at the
+    # build-tree paths the linker records, this plug-in would drag in a SECOND
+    # copy of each and register its protocol there, where the application never
+    # looks: it probes and loads without a single error and WhatsApp simply
+    # never connects. Rewriting the references to the bundle's frameworks makes
+    # it share one instance, like every other bundled protocol plug-in. The
+    # ad-hoc codesign afterwards is what keeps Xcode's copy phase from
+    # stripping the Go runtime out of the binary on its way into the bundle.
+    add_custom_command(TARGET ${TARGET_NAME} POST_BUILD
+        COMMAND install_name_tool
+            -change "${PURPLE_LIBRARY_DIRS}/libpurple.0.dylib" "@executable_path/../Frameworks/libpurple.framework/Versions/0/libpurple"
+            -change "${PURPLE_LIBRARY_DIRS}/libglib-2.0.0.dylib" "@executable_path/../Frameworks/libglib.framework/Versions/2.0.0/libglib"
+            -change "${PURPLE_LIBRARY_DIRS}/libgobject-2.0.0.dylib" "@executable_path/../Frameworks/libgobject.framework/Versions/2.0.0/libgobject"
+            -change "/opt/homebrew/opt/gettext/lib/libintl.8.dylib" "@executable_path/../Frameworks/libintl.framework/Versions/8/libintl"
+            "$<TARGET_FILE:${TARGET_NAME}>"
+        COMMAND codesign --force --sign - "$<TARGET_FILE:${TARGET_NAME}>"
+        COMMENT "Pointing ${TARGET_NAME} at the app bundle's libpurple and glib, then signing")
 endif()
 
 if (WIN32)
